<v SPEAKER_08>that you're asking we as a city of lancaster already take very good care of the infrastructure that we currently have i did we've identified certainly within the stormwater infrastructure that in order to be able to utilize it as a tool for reducing the requirements of the consent decree we need to make sure that we are maintaining that green infrastructure at a level that is predictably performing at levels that we can pre that we can predict um we're for the last 15 to 25 years we've been replacing old infrastructure slowly but surely replacing transit lines for the last 10 to 15 years we've also been replacing lead service lines previous to the lead and copper rule however there's a lot of old infrastructure some of it as much as almost 200 years old that often needs to be replaced never mind the fact that there's the the large diameter pipe which is large largely made of steel and it has ex it has exceeded its useful life several years ago so we're we are continuing to make those replacements for for its benefit having said all of that we predictably without unfunded mandate requirements we are probably in a very good position to continue to manage and maintain the infrastructure that we currently have the big monkey wrench that gets thrown into the works is having to deal with these unfunded mandates the lead service line replacements on an incredibly short timeframe of approximately 10 years to replace rather than what originally had been asked for, which was 30 years, is what may make the cost of doing the lead service lines very difficult to reach. And that's one of the reasons why we worked so hard to work with the public to self-identify these lines so that we had a better sense going before the deadlines of what the likely amount of lead service lines to be replaced will be. We still don't know that full number. We're about to get new information from what we call machine learning over the course of the next several months, which hopefully will drastically reduce that amount. But we have to be conservative and figure that any line, and in fact the feds tell us, line that is not already identified as lead or is not lead must be assumed to be lead. So the more we identify the lead lines and the ones that are not lead, the better we are financially. The last and most unpredictable and trickiest and most expensive of these costs is going to be dealing with PFAS, PFOA. Only about, I would say, 20 to 25 percent of the nation has PFOA challenges in the way that the old cities of the Northeast and old cities in the Midwest have. A lot of it is because we have PFOS, PFOS was used in fertilizers and in pesticides for a long time, much of that has been discontinued, however PFOS is still continued to be used in plastics in many of the fire reduction clothing and the chemicals that are used for other purposes. And the regulations are not imposing themselves on the source of PFAS. They're imposing themselves on the water system. 20% of the PFAS you are exposed to comes through the water systems. The rest of it does come through plastics, through the air, through soil, through other means of production, and you may actually have more access to PFAS if you drink your water through plastic water bottles all the time than using the water out of your tap. However, the regulations are very strong. The state of Pennsylvania up until now has had really good regulations, 14 parts per trillion has been one of the most aggressive of the standards of the states in the country and we have been able to stay within that limit except for periods of severe drought as we had over this last year. If the standard goes to four parts per trillion, then it really becomes an all bets are off situation where one has to look at extraordinarily expensive options and we would need to very strongly seek other kinds of funding and support we would need federal support we need all kinds of other ways of being able to take that on and what you'll see as we've talked about it in terms of the long-term control plan is we're also trying to space out the requested funding for the long-term for the consent decree initiatives so that it minimizes or does not overturn the apple cart in terms of how much it's going to cost over time to take care of these infrastructure matters. So the big items that are unpredictable are the lead service lines and the PFAS, PFOA. Other than that, we are generally in very good shape in terms of being able to manage infrastructure in a way that that can allow us to be much more predictable about the costs involved

